\details{
\strong{Which ordination?} \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{mds()}, and \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}} all fit a
configuration to \code{dm} in \code{n_axes} dimensions. \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}} (classical scaling) is exact and fast, has
eigenvalues and "variance explained", but fits the squared distances after
double centering, so large distances dominate, and non-Euclidean
distances such as Bray-Curtis lose part of their structure to negative
eigenvalues. \code{mds()} fits the distances themselves, in their own units,
so the plot's distances can be read as the data's, with \code{stress} saying
how well. \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}} fits only their rank order, which suits distances
whose values matter less than their order, such as the saturated
Bray-Curtis values of very different samples; it is the usual choice for
community data. \code{mds()} and \code{nmds()} iterate from several starts and take
longer than \code{pcoa()}.

\strong{Method}

Metric MDS minimizes the raw stress
\eqn{\sigma = \sum_{i<j}(d_{ij} - \delta_{ij})^2}, where \eqn{d_{ij}} are
the distances between points on the plot and \eqn{\delta_{ij}} the
distances in \code{dm}. It does so by SMACOF (de Leeuw, 1977; Borg & Groenen,
2005), as scikit-learn's metric \code{smacof()} does: each iteration moves the
points by the Guttman transform, \eqn{X \leftarrow \Gamma(X) =
\frac{1}{n} B(X) X}, which never increases \eqn{\sigma}. A start stops
when an iteration lowers \eqn{\sigma} by no more than \code{tol} times
\eqn{\sum d_{ij}^2}, or after \code{max_iters} iterations.

As in \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}} (see there), iterations take relaxed steps,
\eqn{X \leftarrow 2\Gamma(X) - X} (de Leeuw & Heiser, 1980), when \code{k > 1},
until one makes no more than \code{tol} progress, and then plain steps until a
plain step does; \code{n_axes = 1} uses plain steps only, and so does the last
iteration allowed by \code{max_iters}. So the returned configuration is a fixed
point of \eqn{\Gamma} to within \code{tol}, at the right scale
(\eqn{\sum d_{ij}\delta_{ij} = \sum d_{ij}^2}), typically in about half the
iterations that plain steps take. With plain steps only (an internal
setting used by the tests), a start follows scikit-learn's iterations from
the same starting configuration to within rounding error (about
\eqn{10^{-13}}, relatively, in every iteration's raw stress).

SMACOF only finds a local minimum of stress, so, as in \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}:
\enumerate{
\item The first start is the PCoA solution, from \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}. For distances
that are Euclidean in \code{n_axes} dimensions, PCoA recovers the configuration,
and SMACOF stops at once. (An axis without a positive eigenvalue gets
small random coordinates instead.)
\item Then come \code{n_starts} random starts, with coordinates uniform on [0, 1).
On beta diversity distances, a random start sometimes ends with a
stress up to a few percent lower than the PCoA start's.
\item The start with the lowest stress is kept; if several tie exactly, the
earliest. Its configuration is centered, rotated to its principal
axes, and each axis is flipped so that its coordinate with the largest
absolute value is positive (the first such sample, if several are
within a relative \eqn{10^{-8}} of it). It is not rescaled: distances
on the plot are in the units of \code{dm}.
}

\strong{Stress}

The \code{stress} reported is
\eqn{\sqrt{\sigma / \sum \delta_{ij}^2}}: the raw stress, normalized by
the distances in \code{dm}. It only depends on \eqn{\sigma}, so the lowest
stress is the best fit, and it doesn't depend on the units of \code{dm}. It is
1 when all points coincide, and at most 1 after any SMACOF iteration
(\code{max_iters = 0} returns the starting configuration, whose stress can be
higher). Pairs with a distance of 0 in \code{dm}, such as duplicate samples,
are pulled towards the same point.

scikit-learn reports \eqn{\sigma} itself by default for metric MDS, or,
with \code{normalized_stress = True}, \eqn{\sqrt{\sigma / \sum d_{ij}^2}}
(normalized by the distances on the plot instead). For a result \code{x}, that
is \code{sqrt(sum((dist(x) - dm)^2) / sum(dist(x)^2))}.

\strong{Differences from scikit-learn}
\itemize{
\item scikit-learn takes plain Guttman steps; \code{mds()} takes relaxed ones
(see above).
\item scikit-learn runs \code{n_init} random starts (\code{init = "random"}, its
current default) or one start from classical MDS (\code{init = "classical_mds"}); \code{mds()} runs both kinds, and ranks them by the stress
described above.
\item scikit-learn's defaults are \code{max_iter = 300} and \code{eps = 1e-6}; \code{mds()}'s
are \code{max_iters = 1000} and \code{tol = 1e-7}.
\item scikit-learn returns the configuration as SMACOF leaves it; \code{mds()}
centers, rotates, and flips it, which doesn't change the stress.
\item scikit-learn stops when stress falls by \emph{less} than \code{eps}; \code{mds()} stops
when it falls by no more than \code{tol}, so that \code{tol = 0} stops once stress
no longer falls.
\item scikit-learn computes distances on the plot with the expansion
\eqn{|x|^2 - 2 x \cdot y + |y|^2}, and replaces distances of 0 by
\eqn{10^{-5}} in the Guttman transform; \code{mds()} computes them directly,
and leaves out pairs at distance 0, whose contribution
\eqn{r_{ij}(x_i - x_j)} is 0 either way.
\item The random starts come from \code{seed}, not from R's or NumPy's random
number generators, so \code{set.seed()} has no effect, and R's random number
stream is left untouched.
}

\strong{Speed and memory}

\code{mds()} shares its C engine with \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, without the sorting and the
isotonic regression: \code{dm} is read in its own order, once converted to a
plain vector of doubles. Each start running at the same time needs
\eqn{256 n k} bytes; nothing of size \eqn{n \times n} is formed, and
there is no limit on the number of samples. Each SMACOF iteration makes one pass over the pairs, which takes
time proportional to \eqn{n^2 k}. Several starts run at once, one per
thread, while one iteration takes under about \eqn{2^{26}} pairs of work
(\eqn{n} up to about 11,500); otherwise starts run one at a time, with
each iteration split across \code{cpus} threads (for at least \eqn{2^{18}}
pairs), in parts between checks for user interrupts.

\strong{Reproducibility}

The result, with all its attributes, is bit-identical from run to run, in
new R sessions, and for any value of \code{cpus}, given the same \code{dm}, \code{n_axes},
\code{n_starts}, \code{max_iters}, \code{tol}, and \code{seed}, for the reasons given in \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}.
\code{dm * 2^p} gives exactly the result for \code{dm}, times \eqn{2^p}. (As for
every ordination, distances above \eqn{10^{150}} are rejected.) Results can
still differ in the last bits between computers or compilers, as for any
floating point code.

\code{n_axes} larger than \eqn{n - 1} is reduced to \eqn{n - 1}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last \code{display},
with the ordination's title for \code{plot()} and \code{print()}: the method's
name, and for the methods that take distances, the distance matrix's
title, such as "Principal Coordinates Analysis of Bray-Curtis
Dissimilarity" (see \link{ecodive_display}). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

